So the management-access filtering surface on this platform is, in full:
**`access-list` + `access-group` on the ingress port** (limb A), **an access-list number
directly on an SNMP community** (limb B), **`ssh server <port>`** (limb C),
**`ssh server allow-users` / `deny-users`**, and **`service telnet|ssh ip|ipv6`** per
address family. The last two are covered elsewhere in this campaign and are discussed under
STEP 7 rather than re-measured here.

## The instrument, and the one change this case made to the shared tooling

A management filter has **three** possible answers and only the wire tells them apart. No tool
in `tools/` could send a crafted TCP SYN, so rather than add a ninth near-duplicate traffic
tool (`tools/README.md` says to extend one), **`tools/arpleg.py` gained a `--tcp` leg** in the
same commit as this case:

```
--tcp DST[:PORT][@SRC][%L2DST][,...]     default port 22
```
It builds `Ether/IP/TCP(flags="S")` with the tool's usual `CKARPLEG-<tag>` marker payload, and
the report now prints the TCP flag combination of every frame, so one leg reads:

| reply flags | meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `SA` | the port is **open** -- the service accepted the connection |
| `R` / `RA` | the port is **refused** -- the host answered, no listener |
| *no reply row at all* | the frame was **silently dropped** by a filter |

`@SRC` and `%L2DST` parse exactly as for `--udp`, so one leg can carry the denied source and the
permitted control. The two-counter guard (`COUNTER DISAGREEMENT`, exit 7) now covers `tcp`
frames too.

🔴 **Why STEP 1 below is a baseline with the filter OFF, and why that is not optional.** Data on
a SYN is legal but unusual. If the DUT had ignored a SYN carrying a payload, every later
silence would have read as a filter when it was really a dead instrument. The ACL-OFF baseline
produces `SA` from the very same frames, which proves the instrument before any silence is
interpreted. This is `campaign-measurement-discipline` ("baseline the feature OFF first") doing
real work, not ceremony.

