## STEP 1 -- baseline with NO filter: both management interfaces answer, and the instrument is proven

Two legs, one per host NIC, with the DUT carrying no access-list at all.

```
sudo arpleg.py --tag base-eth3 --out /tmp/mgmt1159/T33386 --iface eth3 \
     --tcp 10.38.22.34:22,10.38.22.34:23 --icmp 10.38.22.34 \
     --sender-ip 10.38.22.33 --sender-mac 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e \
     --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --count 4 --wait 4
```
```
  cap base-eth3-eth3.pcap: 30 frame(s)
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :22    S    [ours]
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.34    :22    -> 10.38.22.33    :40000 SA
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :22    R
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :23    S    [ours]
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.34    :23    -> 10.38.22.33    :40000 RA
    x4     ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.33     -> 10.38.22.34     [ours]
    x4     ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.34     -> 10.38.22.33     [ours]
    x1     ARP-REQ  who-has 10.38.22.34     tell 10.38.22.33
    x1     ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.34     is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43
```
```
sudo arpleg.py --tag base-eth4 ... --iface eth4 --tcp 10.38.22.66:22 --icmp 10.38.22.66 \
     --sender-ip 10.38.22.65 --sender-mac 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --count 4
```
```
  cap base-eth4-eth4.pcap: 22 frame(s)
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.65    :40000 -> 10.38.22.66    :22    S    [ours]
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.66    :22    -> 10.38.22.65    :40000 SA
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.65    :40000 -> 10.38.22.66    :22    R
    x4     ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65     -> 10.38.22.66     [ours]
    x4     ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.66     -> 10.38.22.65     [ours]
```

**Reading it.** 4 of 4 SYNs to each management interface's TCP/22 were answered `SA`: both are
**open**. 4 of 4 SYNs to TCP/23 were answered `RA`: the telnet server is **off**, which is the
documented baseline for this unit -- and it is in this leg on purpose, because it shows what a
*refused service* looks like, so that a *filtered* frame later cannot be confused with it.
4 of 4 ICMP echoes were answered. (The `R` rows are the testbox kernel resetting a SYN-ACK for a
connection its own stack never opened; that is expected with a crafted SYN and does not affect
the measurement.)

**STEP 1 verdict: PASS (baseline established, instrument proven).**

## STEP 2 -- 🔥 FINDING: a NUMBERED hardware ACL silently keeps only ONE rule

The design for limb A was a three-rule numbered list: permit the specified host, then deny
TCP/22 to each management subnet. Sent through `cfg.py`, which stops on any `% ` line:

```
awplus(config)#access-list 3010 permit tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22
awplus(config)#access-list 3010 deny tcp any 10.38.22.32/27 eq 22
awplus(config)#access-list 3010 deny tcp any 10.38.22.64/27 eq 22
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.10
awplus(config-if)#access-group 3010
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.48
awplus(config-if)#access-group 3010
```
**Every command was accepted. Not one `% ` line was printed.** The tool exited 0.

What the device actually holds:

```
awplus#show access-list
Hardware IP access list 3010
    4 deny tcp any 10.38.22.64/27 eq 22

awplus#show running-config | include access-list
access-list 3010 deny tcp any 10.38.22.64/27 eq 22
```

🔥 **Only the LAST of the three rules exists. The permit and the first deny are gone, with no
error, no warning and nothing in the running-config.** A numbered hardware ACL on this platform
holds exactly one rule: each `access-list <n> <rule>` **replaces** the previous one.

**Why this matters more than it looks.** A management filter is by definition
permit-the-specified-host-then-deny-the-rest. That is at least two rules. Written the obvious
way with a numbered list it **silently collapses to the last rule entered** -- which is the
*deny*. A filter built this way would deny the administrator's own host too, and the only
warning the operator gets is a `show access-list` they may never run. This is the same family as
the defects already in `platforms/x960.md` section 5 (`show mirror` reporting a mirror that
copies nothing): **the device's own answer about itself is not the measurement.**

**The device's own log agrees, at `err` severity.** `show log` for this minute (DUT UTC
01:00:18 = **14:00:18 NZDT**, exactly the moment the three rules were entered):

```
2026 Oct 21 01:00:18 local7.err awplus EXFX[1015]: DBG:exfx_cExt_dxch_rules_pclIdActionUpdate 7076: Didn't find matching rule (to id 1) for the copying from one (from id 0)
```

The hardware-ACL driver logged an error about a second rule slot that was not there, at the
instant the second rule should have been installed. That is independent corroboration from
inside the product, not an inference from the CLI.

**The dataplane effect of the one surviving rule, measured.** The surviving rule denies TCP/22
to `10.38.22.64/27`, i.e. to `vlan48 10.38.22.66`, from any source. `vlan10 10.38.22.34` is in
`10.38.22.32/27`, which the surviving rule does not match, so the hardware ACL's **implicit
permit** leaves it open.

```
sudo arpleg.py --tag num-eth4 ... --iface eth4 --tcp 10.38.22.66:22 --icmp 10.38.22.66 ...
  cap num-eth4-eth4.pcap: 12 frame(s)
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.65    :40000 -> 10.38.22.66    :22    S    [ours]
    x4     ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65     -> 10.38.22.66     [ours]
    x4     ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.66     -> 10.38.22.65     [ours]

sudo arpleg.py --tag num-eth3 ... --iface eth3 --tcp 10.38.22.34:22 --icmp 10.38.22.34 ...
  cap num-eth3-eth3.pcap: 22 frame(s)
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :22    S    [ours]
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.34    :22    -> 10.38.22.33    :40000 SA
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :22    R
    x4     ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.33     -> 10.38.22.34     [ours]
    x4     ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.34     -> 10.38.22.33     [ours]
```

| flow | sent | answered | reading |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| eth4 -> `10.38.22.66:22` (the denied destination) | **4 SYN** | **0** -- no reply row at all | **silently dropped in hardware**. Compare the same 4 frames in STEP 1, which got 4 `SA` |
| eth4 -> `10.38.22.66` ICMP echo (**control, must NOT match**) | 4 | **4 echo replies** | the port, the link and the SVI are alive; the filter is **selective**, not a dead interface |
| eth3 -> `10.38.22.34:22` (**control, a different destination subnet**) | 4 SYN | **4 `SA`** | unaffected, as the rule's destination does not match -- the hardware ACL's implicit PERMIT |

And the device's own counter agrees exactly with the wire:
```
awplus#show access-list counters
Name: 3010
	Rule: deny tcp any 10.38.22.64/27 eq 22
	Packet Hits: 4
```
(0 before the leg, 4 after -- the four SYNs.)

**STEP 2 verdict: PASS as a measurement (a filter demonstrably denies management traffic in the
dataplane), with a 🔥 FINDING against the numbered-ACL form.**

## STEP 3 -- the correct instrument: a NAMED hardware ACL, permit-then-deny, is the literal TPS 6.1.1 filter

The named sub-mode is the form that holds more than one rule. Applied with `cfg.py`, after
unbinding and deleting 3010:

```
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.10
awplus(config-if)#no access-group 3010
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.48
awplus(config-if)#no access-group 3010
awplus(config)#no access-list 3010
awplus(config)#access-list hardware ckmgmt
awplus(config-ip-hw-acl)#permit tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22
awplus(config-ip-hw-acl)#deny tcp any any eq 22
awplus(config-ip-hw-acl)#exit
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.10
awplus(config-if)#access-group ckmgmt
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.48
awplus(config-if)#access-group ckmgmt
```
No `% ` line. And this time **both rules are there, in the order entered**:

```
awplus#show access-list
Hardware IP access list ckmgmt
    4 permit tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22
    8 deny tcp any any eq 22

awplus#show running-config | include access
access-list hardware ckmgmt
 permit tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22
 deny tcp any any eq 22
 access-group ckmgmt        [under port1.0.10]
 access-group ckmgmt        [under port1.0.48]
```
That is the direct A/B that pins the STEP 2 finding: **the same two-rule intent, entered in the
named sub-mode, is kept in full; entered in the numbered form, all but the last is discarded.**

This list is exactly TPS 6.1.1's sentence: *permit the user specified host, deny everything else
addressed to the management interface on that service.*

### The measurement

```
sudo arpleg.py --tag named-eth4 ... --iface eth4 --tcp 10.38.22.66:22 --icmp 10.38.22.66 \
     --sender-ip 10.38.22.65 --sender-mac 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --count 4
  cap named-eth4-eth4.pcap: 12 frame(s)
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.65    :40000 -> 10.38.22.66    :22    S    [ours]
    x4     ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65     -> 10.38.22.66     [ours]
    x4     ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.66     -> 10.38.22.65     [ours]

sudo arpleg.py --tag named-eth3 ... --iface eth3 --tcp 10.38.22.34:22,10.38.22.34:23 \
     --icmp 10.38.22.34 --sender-ip 10.38.22.33 --sender-mac 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e ... --count 4
  cap named-eth3-eth3.pcap: 30 frame(s)
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :22    S    [ours]
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.34    :22    -> 10.38.22.33    :40000 SA
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :22    R
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :23    S    [ours]
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.34    :23    -> 10.38.22.33    :40000 RA
    x4     ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.33     -> 10.38.22.34     [ours]
    x4     ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.34     -> 10.38.22.33     [ours]
```

**The permit and the block, side by side, both counted, in one configuration:**

| flow | sent | answered | verdict |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **PERMIT** -- the user specified host `10.38.22.33` -> management `:22` | 4 SYN | **4 `SA`** | the specified host reaches the management service |
| **BLOCK** -- the non-specified host `10.38.22.65` -> management `:22` | 4 SYN | **0, no reply of any kind** | denied, silently, in hardware |
| **control 1** -- the non-specified host's ICMP to the same management address | 4 | **4 echo replies** | the deny is **service-specific**, not a dead interface, and the capture is alive |
| **control 2** -- the specified host to `:23`, a service that is OFF | 4 SYN | **4 `RA`** | a **refused service answers**; a **filtered frame does not**. The two blocks are distinguishable on the wire, so "it was blocked" is not being read off a single ambiguous signal |

And the counters, read after the legs:
```
awplus#show access-list counters
Name: ckmgmt
	Rule: permit tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22
	Packet Hits: 8
	Rule: deny tcp any any eq 22
	Packet Hits: 4
```
8 permit hits = the 4 SYNs plus the 4 kernel RSTs from `10.38.22.33` (both are TCP from that
host to dport 22, so both match the permit rule). 4 deny hits = the 4 SYNs from `10.38.22.65`.
**The device's counters and the wire agree frame for frame.**

**STEP 3 verdict: PASS. TPS 6.1.1's first statement is satisfied and proven in the dataplane.**

## STEP 4 -- removal: the filter is gone from the configuration AND from the dataplane

The step text is the floor; `satisfactory-level-of-testing` requires removal to be proved too.

```
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.10
awplus(config-if)#no access-group ckmgmt
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.48
awplus(config-if)#no access-group ckmgmt
awplus(config)#no access-list hardware ckmgmt
```
```
awplus#show access-list                       <- empty, no list at all
awplus#show running-config | include access
 switchport mode access ...                   <- the only `access` lines left are switchport ones;
                                                 no access-list, no access-group
```
```
sudo arpleg.py --tag removed-eth4 ... --iface eth4 --tcp 10.38.22.66:22 ... --count 4
  cap removed-eth4-eth4.pcap: 12 frame(s)
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.65    :40000 -> 10.38.22.66    :22    S    [ours]
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.66    :22    -> 10.38.22.65    :40000 SA
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.65    :40000 -> 10.38.22.66    :22    R
```
The previously denied host is answered **4 of 4 `SA`** again. **The arc closed on the wire, not
just in the configuration.**

**STEP 4 verdict: PASS.**

## STEP 5 -- a second, independent filter mechanism: an access-list number on an SNMP community

This limb matters because TPS 6.1.1 names SNMP explicitly, and because it uses the **opposite
implicit action**: `access-list 10` is a **standard** list, which implicitly **DENIES** what it
does not permit (`platforms/x960.md` section 5). Grading this limb with the hardware-ACL habit
would get it exactly backwards.

```
awplus(config)#access-list 10 permit 10.38.22.33 0.0.0.0
awplus(config)#snmp-server
awplus(config)#snmp-server community ckacl ro 10        <- the filtered community
awplus(config)#snmp-server community ckopen ro          <- the CONTROL community, no ACL
```
(`snmp-server` was already present in the running-config before this case, so that line changed
nothing; it is **not** removed at teardown.)

```
awplus#show access-list
Standard IP access list 10
    4 permit 10.38.22.33

awplus#show running-config | include snmp-server
snmp-server
snmp-server community ckacl 10
snmp-server community ckopen
```
Measured detail: **`ro` is the default and is not stored** -- `snmp-server community ckacl ro 10`
reads back as `snmp-server community ckacl 10`. A grep for `ro` would not find it.

### The 2x2 matrix, run with `tools/snmpq.py`, which classifies the outcome rather than returning a bare exit code

`snmpq.py get <agent> 1.3.6.1.2.1.1.5.0 --v2c --community <c> --timeout 3 --retries 1`.
The source address is decided by the route: querying `10.38.22.34` leaves from `eth3`
(`10.38.22.33`), querying `10.38.22.66` leaves from `eth4` (`10.38.22.65`).

| source | community | result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `10.38.22.33` (**permitted** by access-list 10) | `ckacl` (ACL-bound) | `RESULT VALUE -- .1.3.6.1.2.1.1.5.0 = STRING: awplus` |
| `10.38.22.65` (**not permitted**) | `ckacl` (ACL-bound) | **`RESULT TIMEOUT -- Timeout: No Response from 10.38.22.66.`** |
| `10.38.22.65` (**not permitted**) | `ckopen` (**control**, no ACL) | `RESULT VALUE -- .1.3.6.1.2.1.1.5.0 = STRING: awplus` |
| `10.38.22.33` | `ckopen` | `RESULT VALUE -- .1.3.6.1.2.1.1.5.0 = STRING: awplus` |

**Exactly one cell of four fails, and it is the one the filter names.** The control community
answers the very same source host over the very same path in the same minute, so the timeout is
the access-list and not a lost packet, a dead agent or an unreachable address -- which is
precisely the ambiguity a bare timeout would otherwise leave.

**The DUT logged the denial itself**, at DUT UTC 01:05:30 = **14:05:30 NZDT**, the instant of the
denied query:
```
2026 Oct 21 01:05:30 kern.err awplus snmpd[1818]: Received SNMP Packet from illegal server 10.38.22.65
2026 Oct 21 01:05:53 kern.err awplus snmpd[525]: Last message 'Received SNMP Packet' repeated 1 times, suppressed by syslog-ng on awplus
```
Note what that tells you about the **mechanism**, and it is a genuinely useful distinction:
the hardware ACL of STEP 3 drops the frame in silicon and the DUT never sees it, while the
SNMP community ACL is enforced **in the agent** -- the packet arrives, is evaluated and is
discarded with a log line. Two different filters, two different places in the stack, the same
externally visible silence.

**STEP 5 verdict: PASS.**

## STEP 6 -- TPS 6.1.1's second statement: the service's TCP port number is configurable

```
awplus(config)#ssh server 2222
awplus#show ssh server
SSH Server                         : Enabled                                    
Protocol                           : IPv4,IPv6                                  
Port                               : 2222                                       
awplus#show running-config | include ssh
ssh server 2222
ssh server allow-users manager
service ssh
```
and on the wire, from the specified host, **both ports probed in one leg**:
```
sudo arpleg.py --tag port2222 ... --iface eth3 --tcp 10.38.22.34:22,10.38.22.34:2222 ... --count 4
  cap port2222-eth3.pcap: 20 frame(s)
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :22    S    [ours]
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.34    :22    -> 10.38.22.33    :40000 RA      <- nothing listens on 22 any more
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :2222  S    [ours]
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.34    :2222  -> 10.38.22.33    :40000 SA      <- the service moved here
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :2222  R
```
**The service really moved**: TCP/22 went from `SA` (STEP 1) to `RA`, and TCP/2222 from no
listener to `SA`, in the same leg, 4 frames each. Had this been graded from `show ssh server`
alone it would have been a read-back, which section 7F.2 of `platforms/x960.md` shows is not
evidence on this platform.

**Restored, and the restore proved on the wire:**
```
awplus(config)#ssh server 22
awplus#show running-config | include ssh
ssh server allow-users manager
service ssh                                   <- the explicit port line is GONE: 22 is the
                                                 default and is not stored
```
```
sudo arpleg.py --tag port-restored ... --iface eth3 --tcp 10.38.22.34:22,10.38.22.34:2222 ... --count 4
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.34    :22    -> 10.38.22.33    :40000 SA      <- back
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.34    :2222  -> 10.38.22.33    :40000 RA      <- gone
```

**STEP 6 verdict: PASS. TPS 6.1.1's second statement is satisfied and proven in the dataplane.**

## STEP 7 -- the rest of the management-filter surface, and what was deliberately NOT exercised

- **`ssh server allow-users` / `ssh server deny-users`** exist (shown in the item-10 probe) and
  the DUT already carries `ssh server allow-users manager`. They were **deliberately not
  exercised**. Driving a deny through them means attempting an authentication that must fail,
  and 🔴 on this bench AW+ locks an account at 5 failed logins with every further failure
  re-arming the 5-minute lock; group A reached 4 on a throwaway account today through a tooling
  bug. The session's standing instruction is that every deliberate auth failure goes against a
  non-existent user or a throwaway account and never against `manager` -- and `allow-users` is a
  *per-account* filter, so a clean negative leg would have to target an existing account. The
  risk of locking the only privileged account on the DUT is not worth a limb that adds nothing
  to the TPS 6.1.1 statements already proven. **Recorded as NOT MEASURED by choice, with the
  reason**, not silently skipped.
- **`service telnet ip|ipv6` and `service ssh ip|ipv6`**, the per-address-family management
  filter, exist and are already measured on this exact platform: `platforms/x960.md` section
  7F.1 (telnet) and section 7F.10 (SSH, measured today in group A's T33380, including that
  IPv4 TCP/22 is answered by `RST.ACK` while `show ssh server` still reads Enabled). Not
  re-measured here; cited.
- 🔴 **`access-class` under `line vty` and `ip access-list` in global config do not exist on
  this platform** -- both measured first-hand above. This is a **negative result of the case**,
  not a gap in it: the ACL guide's generic AW+ statement that "standard lists still bind to vty
  access-class" does not hold on this build, and a management-ACL design copied from that
  sentence cannot be configured at all.

## STEP 8 -- reboot persistence: NOT MEASURED, and why

`satisfactory-level-of-testing` asks for persistence across a reboot as well as removal.
**It is not measured here, and it is not skipped silently.**

Proving that an `access-group` survives a reboot requires `write`. On swi_d a `write` would
save the whole running-config, **including vlan 60**, which the sentinel directed be kept
**unsaved** as campaign infrastructure for groups C and D. This is the identical bind group A
recorded for T33380. Spending a DUT reload on it now would also not be free: it would clear
vlan 60 and force the next group to rebuild it.

**Status: NOT MEASURED, reason recorded, reported to the sentinel as a
`NEEDS TEST ENGINEER:` line.** The campaign-end teardown, which removes vlan 60 anyway, is the
cheap place to run it.

