## Findings for `platforms/x960.md`

1. 🔥 **A NUMBERED hardware ACL (3000-3699) holds exactly ONE rule on this platform.** Each
   `access-list <n> <rule>` silently REPLACES the previous one -- no error, no warning, nothing
   in the running-config to show a rule was discarded. Three rules entered, one kept (the last).
   The **named** sub-mode (`access-list hardware <name>`) keeps them all, in order. Since every
   management filter is permit-then-deny, the numbered form **cannot express one**, and written
   the obvious way it collapses to the deny -- locking out the administrator's own host. Pairs
   with the existing section 5 note that a bound numbered list cannot be edited while a named
   one can; the underlying fact is that the numbered form is single-entry.
2. **The hardware-ACL driver logs it, at `err`:** `local7.err ... EXFX[...]:
   DBG:exfx_cExt_dxch_rules_pclIdActionUpdate 7076: Didn't find matching rule (to id 1) for the
   copying from one (from id 0)`, at the instant the second rule should have been installed.
3. **`access-class` does not exist under `line vty` on this build** (`acc ?` offers only
   `login`), and **`ip access-list` does not exist in global config**. The ACL guide's generic
   "standard lists still bind to vty access-class" does **not** hold here.
4. **Two filters, two layers, one appearance.** A hardware `access-group` deny is enforced in
   silicon and the DUT logs nothing; an SNMP community access-list is enforced in `snmpd`, which
   logs `kern.err ... Received SNMP Packet from illegal server <ip>`. Both look like silence to
   the client. The log line is the cheap way to tell a software-filtered query from a lost one.
5. **`snmp-server community <c> ro <acl>` stores as `snmp-server community <c> <acl>`** -- `ro`
   is the default and is not written back. A grep for `ro` finds nothing on a read-only
   community.
6. **`ssh server <port>` is stored only when it is not 22.** Setting 2222 writes
   `ssh server 2222`; setting it back to 22 removes the line entirely.
7. **Expected values, so the next run compares instead of discovering** (tb277 swi_d,
   `sin_x96056-20261019-27`): TCP/22 open = `SA`; TCP/22 with the service moved away, or TCP/23
   with telnet off = `RA`, 4 of 4; a hardware-ACL denied SYN = **no reply row at all**; ICMP
   echo to a management SVI = 4 of 4 replies whether or not a TCP filter is bound; an SNMP get
   of `1.3.6.1.2.1.1.5.0` = `STRING: awplus`.

## VERDICT

**VERDICT: PASS -- the DUT implements a management access filter and both halves of TPS 6.1.1
are proven in the dataplane: a named hardware ACL bound to the management ingress ports admitted
the user specified host (4 of 4 SYNs answered SYN-ACK) while silently dropping the
non-specified host (4 of 4 SYNs unanswered) with an ICMP control from that same denied host
answered 4 of 4 in the same leg, the device's own hit counters agreeing frame for frame; an
access-list number bound to an SNMP community denied the non-specified source while a control
community answered it over the same path in the same minute; and a management service's TCP
port number was moved from 22 to 2222 and back, each state proved on the wire. Every block was
counted and distinguished from a refused service (`RA`) and from a dead interface. Two findings
are recorded: a numbered hardware ACL silently keeps only its last rule, and neither
`access-class` under `line vty` nor `ip access-list` exists on this platform. Reboot persistence
is NOT MEASURED, because proving it needs a `write` that would save the campaign's deliberately
unsaved vlan 60.**
